{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261027T054155Z-d92d0273", "ts": "2026-10-27T05:46:14Z", "actor": {"worktree": "prawduct", "plugin": "3.8.0-dev.1", "branch": "feature/1087-flaky-rerun"}, "body": {"base_commit": "e5fe917a0440cc88d7e3f129cfb05d5fa8f71495", "base_tree": "a8b73aa71ca45161ece4a534bfa0a59f6876ff36", "head_tree": "68873361245d3fe20c689f9477a872733dcbbc06", "head_commit": "927d23c7b1295cf6bea9aa6995f26f7428d94463", "dispatch_commit": "927d23c7b1295cf6bea9aa6995f26f7428d94463", "mode": "cumulative (bundle review, ready for merge)", "mode_chosen_by": "explicit-args", "stage": "boundary", "tier": "escalate", "roster": [{"role": "correctness", "model": "claude-opus-5-5[1m]"}, {"role": "design", "model": "claude-opus-5-5[1m]"}, {"role": "sustainability", "model": "claude-opus-5-5[1m]"}], "files_reviewed": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/docs/project-state-keys.md", "plugin/docs/test-report-contract.md", "plugin/lib/briefing.py", "plugin/lib/gates.py", "plugin/lib/pr_payload.py", "plugin/methodology/building.md", "plugin/skills/critic/goals-1-3.md", "plugin/skills/critic/review-protocol.md", "plugin/skills/pr/SKILL.md", "plugin/templates/project-state.yaml", "tests/test_finding_scope_rule.py", "tests/test_pr_review_payload.py", "tests/test_reviewer_payload_budget.py", "tests/test_test_evidence_flaky.py", "tests/test_v5_methodology.py"], "files_changed": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-flaky-rerun.md", ".prawduct/changes/flaky-rerun.md", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/docs/project-state-keys.md", "plugin/docs/test-report-contract.md", "plugin/lib/briefing.py", "plugin/lib/gates.py", "plugin/lib/pr_payload.py", "plugin/methodology/building.md", "plugin/skills/critic/goals-1-3.md", "plugin/skills/critic/review-protocol.md", "plugin/skills/pr/SKILL.md", "plugin/templates/project-state.yaml", "tests/test_finding_scope_rule.py", "tests/test_pr_review_payload.py", "tests/test_reviewer_payload_budget.py", "tests/test_test_evidence_flaky.py", "tests/test_v5_methodology.py"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-flaky-rerun.md", ".prawduct/changes/flaky-rerun.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "blocking", "title": "record-lint chunk-ref-missing: chunk 02 declared deliverable `tests/test_x.py` does not exist", "recommendation": "Relayed from the manifest's record_lint (chunk-ref-missing is BLOCKING; the relay keeps its severity). Plan line 219 is a Tests bullet giving a node-id example, `tests/test_x.py::TestY::test_z[a]`, and the lint read the `path.py::suffix` token as a declared file. So this is a lint misread, not a dropped deliverable. Scope is class: any plan that backticks a pytest node id gets the same false blocker. Close it either way. Instance: rephrase the example on line 219 so it does not carry a backticked repo-path token (for example, describe the shape without the backticks). Class: have the chunk-ref extractor strip a `::...` suffix before it checks that the file exists. The class fix ends the problem for every plan that quotes node ids.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-flaky-rerun.md"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "A command's original non-test failure is masked when its test failures all pass on retry", "recommendation": "In cmd_test_evidence, when every failure of a command passes on retry, `run_returncodes[root_index] = rc` replaces that command's exit status with the last rerun's. Suppose the full run exited nonzero for two reasons: a test failure plus a non-test cause, such as `--cov-fail-under` or a plugin error over the full suite. The rerun template runs only the failing node ids, so it does not repeat the non-test cause and typically exits 0. The record is then green, and the unexplained-exit check never sees the original nonzero. The plan's mid-build decision says the opposite ('A non-test failure such as a coverage threshold still exits nonzero and is refused as before'). That only holds if the rerun template repeats the threshold, and over a subset of tests it then always fails. Instance: either keep the original exit code unless it matches the runner's test-failure code, or record the masking as an accepted tradeoff in the plan and change record and correct the decision text. Add a test where the full run exits nonzero for a non-test reason alongside a flake.", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "A JUnit `file` attribute naming no file in this repo is judged `touched: false` (the lenient answer)", "recommendation": "`_pytest_node_id` returns a report's `file` attribute as-is, without checking `(project_dir / file).is_file()`. `tests/test_test_evidence_flaky.py::TestPytestNodeIds::test_the_file_attribute_wins` pins exactly this with a file that does not exist. `_flaky_entry` copies the attribute as-is too. `_judge_flakes` then computes `test_file in changed`. A path that is relative to a different pytest rootdir, or absolute (several non-Surefire JUnit writers emit absolute paths), never matches the repo-relative changed set, so it reads False. `block` then credits a flake the branch may have touched, and `warn` draws no Critic warning. That contradicts the plan's 'unknown file reads as touched' and the 'Authority fails closed' conformance claim. Instance: in `_judge_flakes`, treat a file that does not resolve to a file under project_dir as unknown (`touched: null`), or normalize absolute paths against project_dir first. Add a test with an absolute path and a nonexistent `file` attribute under `block`.", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "tests/test_test_evidence_flaky.py"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "The `test-run` fact's `flaky` field is produced but nothing reads it", "recommendation": "Both `cmd_test_evidence` and `_record_narrowed_run` write `run_fact[\"flaky\"]`, to serve the plan's consumer question 4 ('Another worktree reusing a test-run fact: the same flake list, so a reused green carries its flakes'). But `gates.saved_flaky_lines` reads only the local `.test-evidence.json`. A grep of plugin/ finds no reader of a fact's `flaky`, and only a test asserts that the fact carries it. So a worktree vouched for by a store fact prints no `flaky:` lines: test-status, the handoff, the PR payload and the Critic's flaky_policy rule all go dark there. A scratch worktree run (DG2), whose only write is the fact, never surfaces its flakes at all. Separately, `saved_flaky_lines` prints the local record's flakes 'whatever its freshness', even when the verdict came from a different run's fact. Close it by construction: render flakes from the run that actually vouched. The `test_status` verdict path already knows that run, so give `saved_flaky_lines` that run, not the local file.", "files": ["plugin/lib/gates.py", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "A malformed `touched` value crashes test-status and the handoff and payload renders", "recommendation": "`gates.flaky_lines` calls `_FLAKY_TOUCH_TAGS.get((e[\"touched\"], ...))`. An unhashable value, such as `\"touched\": []` or `{}`, raises TypeError. `flaky_entries` checks only that `id` is a string, and a restamp copies prior entries verbatim, so the bad value persists. The test-status exit code then becomes a traceback exit 1 (read as stale), which breaks the docstring's and Chunk 01's 'tolerant of malformed values ... never changes the exit code' contract. `test_a_malformed_flaky_value_changes_nothing` covers only malformed lists and ids, not a malformed `touched`. Instance: look up the tag only when `touched` is in (True, False, None), and add `{\"id\": \"x\", \"touched\": []}` to that test's bad values.", "files": ["plugin/lib/gates.py", "tests/test_test_evidence_flaky.py"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "note", "title": "Chunk 02's product verification on this repo is not evidenced", "recommendation": "Chunk 02's acceptance criterion asks for a forced flaky test run through a temporary `test_rerun_command` on this repo to record green with one flake. The plan's Level names that same run as what would raise it. No commit message or plan entry records it being done. `TestAgainstRealPytest` exercises the same path end to end with real pytest in a temporary repo, which covers the mechanism, so this is likely only a missing record. Either state the run's result in the plan, or record that the real-pytest test stands in for it.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-flaky-rerun.md"], "fid": "R-6"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "severity": "note", "title": "The fallback pytest rerun drops the run's `--` extra args", "recommendation": "The fallback run's argv is `pytest --junit-xml=... -q *extra`, but `_rerun_failures` builds `pytest --junit-xml=... -q *nodes` without `extra`. Options that change collection or ids, such as `-c`, `--rootdir` or `-p` plugin toggles, apply to the first run but not to the retry. The retry can then select nothing, which is safe because the test stays red, or run under different plugins than the green it credits. Instance: pass the non-path entries of `extra` through to the rerun, or document that the retry runs with pytest's defaults.", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "fid": "R-7"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "Generality: the touched judgment can only resolve Python test files, so every flake from a non-Python runner reads as touched", "recommendation": "Class. Why it broke: `_judge_flakes` works out a flake's file only through `_pytest_node_id`, which looks for `<dotted classname>.py`. Surefire's JUnit (the runner Part 2's `<flakyFailure>` ingest exists to serve) puts no `file` attribute on a testcase, and `com.foo.BarTest` never resolves to a `.py`. So every Surefire flake gets `touched: null`, which is read as touched. Under `warn` every such flake earns a boundary WARNING. Under `block` every one is counted as failed whether or not the branch touched it. That contradicts the project-state-keys.md sentence 'A flake the branch did not touch is noted under every policy', and the plan's claim that it conforms to 'Never specific to Python'. Fix: make file resolution language-neutral, for example by matching the classname's path segments against files of any extension under `tests_dirs`. Otherwise state in project-state-keys.md and the plan that for runners whose reports name no file, every flake counts as touched and `block` therefore blocks all of them. Search `_pytest_node_id` callers for other places where a non-pytest id reaches it.", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/docs/project-state-keys.md"], "fid": "R-8"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "flaky_policy is applied in two places at two times; the Critic's half reads a value the evidence does not carry", "recommendation": "Instance. `block` is applied at record time and stored on the entry (`blocked`). The `warn`/`accept` split is left to the reviewer, whose Goal 1 clause says 'under `flaky_policy: warn` (default) -> WARNING at the boundary, else an observation'. But `flaky_lines` (the test-status, PR-payload and handoff output) prints the touched tag and never the policy. A reviewer therefore has to open project-state.yaml, the clause does not tell them to, and the policy there may have changed since the record was made. In practice a repo set to `accept` gets touched flakes warned on, because 'default' is the only value visible. 'else an observation' is also ambiguous: it can mean a non-`warn` policy or the inner stage, and under `accept` the docs say the flake is only noted. Fix: stamp the policy in force on each judged entry at record time (or in the record), and render it in the tag (e.g. `[touched by this branch; flaky_policy warn]`). Then the clause can match on the printed line alone, with the policy decided in one place at one time. Pin it with the existing tag-vs-rule test.", "files": ["plugin/lib/gates.py", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/skills/critic/goals-1-3.md", "plugin/skills/critic/review-protocol.md"], "fid": "R-9"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "A live run's outcome is now rebuilt from five parallel counters edited at three sites; block's red goes in as a fake per-command exit code", "recommendation": "Instance (in cmd_test_evidence). These signals must agree: `passed`, `failed`, `failed_ids`, `failed_by_root` and `run_returncodes`. Classification sets them. The rerun loop adjusts four of them. The `block` branch then changes `passed`/`failed`/`failed_ids` without touching `failed_by_root`, and appends a `1` to `run_returncodes`, a list declared as 'one per executed command'. No combination that can occur today is wrong, because `failed_by_root` and the per-command zip are read before the block step. But the next edit that reads either one after that point gets counts that disagree, and the declaration comment is now false. Fix: put one per-test outcome model (pass / fail / flaked / blocked, per root) in a single place, and derive the counts, `failed_ids` and the exit status from it. Or, at minimum, keep the policy's red as its own flag next to `run_returncodes` rather than inside it.", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "fid": "R-10"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "The handoff depends on the formatter's 'flaky: ' prefix by stripping it", "recommendation": "Instance. `render_session_handoff` calls `line.removeprefix('flaky: ')` on `saved_flaky_lines` output. If the prefix in `flaky_lines` changes, the handoff keeps a doubled label and no test notices. Fix: have the formatter return the body and let each surface add its own label, or add a `prefix=` argument. A small change; no edit is needed if the format is considered frozen.", "files": ["plugin/lib/briefing.py", "plugin/lib/gates.py"], "fid": "R-11"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "warning", "title": "_judge_flakes drops the reason the branch diff could not be read, so under `block` every flake turns red and the output never says why", "recommendation": "Instance. Why it broke: `_judge_flakes` discards the reason from `cov._coverage_resolve_base` (`base, _ = ...`) and catches RuntimeError/OSError/SubprocessError from `_coverage_changed_files` into `changed = None` without printing anything. Every entry then gets `touched: null`. Treating that as touched is the right direction, but nothing the operator reads separates 'the diff could not be read' from 'this test file could not be resolved'. Both print `[branch touch unknown]`, plus `BLOCKED ... counted as failed` under `block`. Consequence: on a shallow CI clone or an unresolvable base, a `flaky_policy: block` repo gets a red record on every flake, and the operator goes looking at test files when the real problem is the base. Rule cited, hook-surface.md: 'Pick each fallback's direction from the invariant it protects: an unknown bucket blocks, degraded advice names its consequence, and a swallow is fixed in the frame that discards it'. Fix: when `changed is None`, print one `note:` line to stderr in `_judge_flakes` giving the base-resolution reason or the exception text, and saying that every flake is treated as touched because of it. Add a test that feeds an unresolvable base and asserts the note.", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "fid": "R-12"}, {"goal": "Learnings Cross-Check", "severity": "warning", "title": "Learnings cross-check: a report `file` attribute relative to a pytest rootdir other than the repo root reads as touched:false, so `block` credits a flake it cannot place", "recommendation": "Instance. This is outside Goals 5 and 6. I raise it because it reintroduces a pattern a rule warns against; correctness should confirm it. Why it broke: `_judge_flakes` checks `test_file in changed` against the report's `file` attribute verbatim (`_flaky_entry` keeps it; the rerun path's `setdefault` keeps it too). `_coverage_changed_files` returns repo-root-relative paths. `_pytest_node_id`'s own comment says a pytest rootdir other than this repo drops leading path segments, and a reporter can also write an absolute path. In both cases the membership test returns False rather than None, so the entry is judged 'outside this branch's diff' and `block` credits it. That is the lenient answer to a question nobody could answer, and it contradicts the plan's tradeoff 'Unknown file reads as touched' and the change fragment's 'null ... is treated as touched by every policy'. Rule cited, hook-surface.md: 'Pick each fallback's direction from the invariant it protects: an unknown bucket blocks'. Fix: normalise the file to repo-relative, and set `touched: null` when the normalised path is not a file under project_dir. Pin it with a test using `file=\"test_x.py\"` for a repo-level `tests/test_x.py` that the branch changed.", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "fid": "R-13"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Mid-build decisions, for the owner", "recommendation": "Scope: none. The plan records four mid-build decisions, each with what settled it. (1) Chunk 02: the rerun runs before the unexplained-exit check, and a fully-flaked command takes its last rerun's exit status. Settled by 'authority fails closed'. (2) Chunk 03: `block` appends its own nonzero return code, and a restamp is not judged again. Settled by 'do it right'. (3) Chunk 03: the untouched tag became `[outside this branch's diff]` so the Critic rule's quoted phrase cannot match it. Caught by the tag-pinning test. (4) Chunk 03: a declared payload raise of about 28 tokens. Settled by NFR 'Review wall-clock', and declared consistently across test_reviewer_payload_budget.py, test_v5_methodology.py and test_finding_scope_rule.py (+29 on the chunk/inner routes). Two other deliberate departures are recorded with a why and a veto: the [DECISION] block on re-implementing reruns against architecture.md's 'never re-implements product tooling', and the tradeoff dropping the option's 'built-in default for pytest/testament'. The plan's five Inferences (retry ceiling, rerun ids, config shape, first_failure, refusal with ingest/restamp) are design parameters, not owner goals, and the code implements each as written. Alignment pass: one batch, three forks, all answered by the owner. No miss found. Not found in the commit messages: evidence for the 'What would raise it' item (Chunk 02 run once on this repo with a forced flake), which is also Chunk 02's product-verification criterion.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-flaky-rerun.md"], "fid": "R-14"}, {"goal": "Backlog Reconciliation", "severity": "note", "title": "Backlog item appears resolved: brookstalley/prawduct#1087 test-evidence: one flaky test forces a full-suite rerun", "recommendation": "Scope: none. Verify it, then call `/prawduct:backlog update brookstalley/prawduct#1087 status=shipped closed-by=scope:flaky-rerun` when the backlog skill's 'When to mark shipped' rule says. The change fragment already carries `closes: [brookstalley/prawduct#1087]`. The deferred covered-code judgment is filed as #1088, which is open and resolves, so the plan's 'File the deferred covered-code backlog item' deliverable is met. `affecting` over the changed code files returned #1079, #672, #755 and #928, and none of them is resolved by this change. C-B4: the ids cited in the records (#1087, #1088) both resolve.", "files": [".prawduct/changes/flaky-rerun.md"], "fid": "R-15"}, {"goal": "Learnings Cross-Check", "severity": "note", "title": "Learnings cross-check and Records Pass coverage", "recommendation": "Scope: none. Read set from `learnings-files --for-diff`: core.md, authoring.md, gates.md, hook-surface.md, pr.md, release.md, reviews.md, tests.md. No rules were added or changed this cycle. The payload and token raises follow authoring.md's 'Pay a prose size ceiling in place ... otherwise declare a raise with its reason': each is declared with a reason. The template comment compression in 927d23c7 pays its own byte cost in place. Records Pass covered both oracle files, .prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-flaky-rerun.md and .prawduct/changes/flaky-rerun.md. One shipping claim clears a bar: the fragment's 'null ... is treated as touched by every policy' is not fully true. That falls under the `file`-attribute warning above and needs no separate edit. Nothing else clears a bar. The plan's Chunk 03 wording `(touched by this branch)` against the shipped bracket tags is narration that a recorded mid-build decision already supersedes. The record-lint `chunk-ref-missing` (`tests/test_x.py`, chunk 02) is relayed by Goal 2, not here. That path is a node-id example inside a Tests bullet.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-flaky-rerun.md", ".prawduct/changes/flaky-rerun.md"], "fid": "R-16"}], "counts": {"blocking": 1, "warning": 9, "note": 6}, "observations": [], "duration_seconds": 540, "scope": "flaky-rerun", "scope_chosen_by": "binding", "scope_unresolved_cause": null, "scope_reason": null, "base_extended_from": null, "chunks": ["01", "02", "03", "04"], "chunks_proposed": ["01", "02", "03", "04"], "chunk": "01", "base_reviewed": "e5fe917a0440cc88d7e3f129cfb05d5fa8f71495", "record_lint": {"records": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-flaky-rerun.md", ".prawduct/changes/flaky-rerun.md", "plugin/docs/project-state-keys.md", "plugin/docs/test-report-contract.md", "plugin/methodology/building.md", "plugin/skills/critic/goals-1-3.md", "plugin/skills/critic/review-protocol.md", "plugin/skills/pr/SKILL.md"], "chunk_graded": "01", "chunks_graded": ["01", "02", "03", "04"], "plan_graded": ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-flaky-rerun.md", "findings": [{"check": "chunk-ref-missing", "path": "chunk 02", "line": 219, "detail": "declared deliverable `tests/test_x.py` file do